CONSTRUCTORS-
A constructor is a special member of a class that is used to initialize an object when the object is created.

class Student {
    String name;
    int age;

    Student() {
        name = "Unknown";
        age = 0;
    }
}

Here- Student() is the Constructor!
When we create an object, Student s1 = new Student(); - 
java automatically calls the Constructor- Student(). So the object's fields get initialized.

WHY WE NEED CONSTRUCTORS-
Suppose we have-
class Student {
    String name;
    int age;
}

we create an object-
Student s1 = new Student();
s1.name = "Raga";
s1.age = 24;
This is Good!
But imagine we have 100 Students-We would repeatedly do:

Student s1 = new Student();
s1.name = "Raga";
s1.age = 24;

Student s2 = new Student();
s2.name = "Sreya";
s2.age = 25;

Student s3 = new Student();
s3.name = "Shrey";
s3.age = 26;

That's lil bit inconvenient.
Here, comes the Constructor- we can use- 

class Student {
    String name;
    int age;

    Student(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }
}

then-
Student s1 = new Student("Raga", 24);
Student s2 = new Student("Sreya", 25);
Student s3 = new Student("Shrey", 26);

NEW CREATES THE OBJECT, AND THE CONSTRUCTOR INITIALIZES IT.

CONSTRUCTOR SYNTAX-
A constructor looks like a method, but there are some important differences.

class Student {

    String name;
    int age;

    Student(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }
}

THE CONSTRUCTOR HAS THE SAME NAME AS THE CLASS.
ALSO CONSTRUCTOR DOES NOT HAVE A RETURN TYPE.

Types of Constructor:-
1) No-argument constructor
2) Parameterized constructor
3) Constructor overloading
4) ***Default constructor

1) No-argument constructor:-
=> A constructor that takes no parameters is called a no-argument constructor.

class Student {

    String name;
    int age;

    Student() {
        name = "Unknown";
        age = 0;
    }
}

2) Parameterized Constructor:-
=> A constructor that accepts parameters is called a parameterized constructor.

class Student {

    String name;
    int age;

    Student(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name; // this line means object's name = parameter's name
        this.age = age;
    }
}
when we do this- 
Student s1 = new Student("Raga", 24);
Student s2 = new Student("Sreya", 25);
Each object gets its own values.

3) Constructor Overloading:-
=> Java allows us to have multiple constructors in the same class, as long as their parameter lists are different.
This is called constructor overloading.

class Student {

    String name;
    int age;

    Student() {
        name = "Unknown";
        age = 0;
    }

    Student(String name) {
        this.name = name;
        age = 0;
    }

    Student(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }
}

here, we have 3 consturctors-
1) Student s1 = new Student(); calls Student()
2) Student s2 = new Student("Raga"); calls Student(String name)
3) Student s3 = new Student("Raga", 24); calls Student(String name, int age)

Java figures out which constructor to call based on the arguments.

4) Default Constructor:-
suppose you write like this, and you didn't write any constructor,-
class Student {
}

-> JAVA AUTOMSTICALLY PROVIDES A CONSTRUCTOR-
Student() {
}
this is called Default Constructor.
so, now this works-Student s1 = new Student();

but...
Suppose if you write your own constructor-
class Student {

    Student(String name) {
        System.out.println(name);
    }
}
now, Student s1 = new Student();  - this will not compile.
Because!- ONCE YOU DEFINE A CONSTRUCTOR YOURSELF, JAVA DOES NOT AUTOMATCALLY PROVIDE THE DEFAULT CONSTRUCTOR.
you would need to explicitly create this( Student s1 = new Student(); )
class Student {

    Student() {
    }

    Student(String name) {
        System.out.println(name);
    }
}
now- both works....
Student s1 = new Student();
Student s2 = new Student("Raga");

DEFAULT CONSTRUCTOR VS NO-ARGUMENT CONSTRUCTOR:-
=> Default constructor
A constructor automatically provided by the compiler when you don't write any constructor.
example-
class Student {
}
Java provides-
Student() {
}
=> No-argument constructor
Any constructor that has zero parameters.
example-
class Student {

    Student() {
        System.out.println("Hello");
    }
}
This is a no-argument constructor, 
but technically it is not the compiler-provided default constructor.

Remember Constructors like this- 
Whenever you see-
Student s = new Student("Raga", 24);
think like this-
                new Student("Raga", 24)
                         |
                         ↓
                 Create Student object
                         |
                         ↓
              Find matching constructor
                         |
                         ↓
              Student(String, int)
                         |
                         ↓
                 Initialize fields
                         |
                         ↓
                    s references
                    that object
-> So the constructor's main job is:Set up the object into a valid initial state.



CONSTRUCTORS CAN CALL OTHER CONSTURCTORS!
Java allows one constructor to call another constructor in the same class using-
this(...)
Think of it as- Hey, call another constructor of this same class.
example-
class Student {

    String name;
    int age;

    Student() {
        this("Unknown", 0);
    }

    Student(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }
}
here, there are two constructors-
1) student()
2) Student(String name, int age)
Suppose if we do- Student s1 = new Student(); -> Student() this constructor gets called!
but inside Student() constructor, we have- this("Unknown", 0); that means-
"Instead of doing the initialization myself, call the other constructor with "Unknown" and 0."
so it goes like this-
new Student()
      ↓
Student()
      ↓
this("Unknown", 0)
      ↓
Student("Unknown", 0)
      ↓
this.name = "Unknown"
this.age = 0

🧾Important Point-  When you use- this(...) - it must be the first statement inside the constructor.
Correct one - ✅
Car() {
    this("Unknown", 0);
} 
Wrong one- ❌
Car() {
    System.out.println("Hello");
    this("Unknown", 0);
}
Thats because- if you're delegating to another constructor, 
do that before doing anything else in this constructor.

2 this's:-
this.name means "the name belonging to the current object."
this.name = name;

this(...) means "call another constructor in the same class."
this("Unknown", 0);

this.name    → current object's variable
this(...)    → another constructor

CONSTRUCTORS DON'T CREATE THE OBJECT-
take:- Student s = new Student("Raga", 24); 
here, "new" is responsible for allocating or creating object.
The "constructor" is responsible for initializing that newly created object.


5️⃣important points about constructors-
1. Constructor has the same name as the class
Student() { }
2. Constructor has no return type
Student() { }       // ✅
void Student() { }  // ❌ constructor; this is a method
3. Constructor runs when an object is created
Student s = new Student();
4. Constructors initialize object state
Student(String name, int age) {
    this.name = name;
    this.age = age;
}
5. If you write no constructor, Java provides a default constructor. 
If you write your own constructor, Java doesn't automatically provide one.














